<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 211 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Gnaeus Scipio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus was a Roman general who landed at Emporion in 218 BCE at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, opening the Roman campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. He defeated Hanno and won over or subdued peoples north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>. With his brother Publius he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> for seven years until both were killed in 211 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Celtiberians served widely as mercenaries: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by Rome deserted <b>Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio</b>, contributing to the destruction of both Scipio brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for Carthage at the Battle of the Great Plains in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tarraco itself had been the base of the brothers <b>Gnaeus</b> and Publius Cornelius Scipio from 218 BCE, and over the next two centuries Roman control spread inland from the Mediterranean coast through the Celtiberian Wars, which ended with the destruction of Numantia in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 218 BCE <b>Gnaeus Scipio</b> defeated the Carthaginian commander Hanno near Cissa, a town sometimes identified with Kese, and the Romans established their base at Tarraco.</blockquote><a href="/state/cessetani">Cessetani</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied with Rome, Emporion was the landing point of <b>Gnaeus Scipio</b> in 218 BCE and of Cato in 195 BCE, when he crushed the revolt of the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/state/emporion">Emporion</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew from Rome's wartime base at Tarraco, established after <b>Gnaeus Scipio's</b> landing in 218 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought the Romans under <b>Gnaeus Scipio</b> in 218 BCE and again in the north-eastern revolt of 197 to 195 BCE, when Cato stormed their chief town.</blockquote><a href="/state/lacetani">Lacetani</a></li></ul></section>
